siberia has long been a byword for the  freezing cold  but this year russian towns across the  arctic circle  have been experiencing a prolonged heat  wave  generating extraordinary temperatures  [Music]  one town was nearly 40 degrees just a  few weeks ago  hard to imagine in years gone by this  was a place where  soviet leaders sent political prisoners  as punishment  because of the plunging mercury in  winter  the heat has triggered out-of-control  wildfires across  thousands of acres of forest prompting  siberia to come under the scrutiny  of a leading group of global scientists  they've run dozens of computer  simulations  to compare the climate in siberia as it  is today with about  one degree of global warming with the  climate as it would have been  without human influence how does this  study offer more  unequivocal evidence that climate change  does exist  compared to other studies the event that  we have looked at  this prolonged heat of a very large part  of of siberia for  um for for for the first six months of  this year  that would have been basically  impossible  to happen without a human induced  climate change  so it is really we are seeing something  new here that we would not have  seen if we had not burned all these  fossil fuels over the last 250 years  the planet is getting warmer this is  svalbard in the arctic  circle known for its frozen tundra and  glaciers which are now  melting fast temperatures in the polo  regions  are rising quickly because oceans carry  heat towards the poles  and reflective ice and snow is melting  away  year after year brings more extreme  weather  not everything can be attributed to  soaring global emissions  but the world weather attribution group  says many  other events like the devastating fires  in australia  six months ago were made more likely  because of climate change  it's all pushing the world towards  another unwelcome record  that it's on course in 2020 to become  the hottest year  ever lisa holland sky news
 

